00:00:05.030 --> 00:00:07.110 align:start position:0%
european union ministers have agreed on  the need to update rules governing the  return of cultural objects unlawfully  removed from a member state  the proposals aim to ensure that any  national treasures such as art or  archaeological findings are swiftly and  efficiently returned to their country of  origin  the ministers supported the idea of  broadening the definition of national  treasures extending deadlines for  initiating return proceedings and  placing the burden of proof on the  possessor of the works of art when it  comes to compensation  at the meeting in brussels the council  also threw its weight behind efforts to  ensure media freedom and pluralism which  the eu considers to be one of the  pillars of democracy  ministers agreed on the need for new  transparency and media ownership  ensuring that journalists are free from  political influence and that their  sources are protected  and on the independence of audio visual  regulators  the council was also briefed on smart tv  as many europeans start to use on-demand  services linking their televisions  computers smartphones and tablets  this evolution in interactive viewing  poses new challenges to the way that  audio-visual media services are  regulated but also to control over  advertising copyright and the protection  of children the ministers debated what  these new developments might mean in  terms of risks for consumers and whether  special regulation might be needed
 

